```dart
test('the fa-tokens line carries the session model id (gh-1460 AC1/AC4)', () async {
  final cli = cliFor([textTurn('ok', usage: reportedUsage())]);
  expect(await cli.runHeadless('say hi'), 0);
  final log = (await env.readTextFile('/home/.fah/logs/fa.log')).valueOrNull!;
  final lines = log.split('\n').where((l) => l.contains('fa-tokens: ')).toList();
  expect(lines, hasLength(1));
  final payload = 'fa-tokens: ${lines.single.split('fa-tokens: ').last}';
  expect(payload, contains('"model":"test-model"'));
  expect(usageTokensLogPattern.hasMatch(payload), isTrue);
});
```

The fake assistant message model is 'test-model' (testAssistant in scripted_stream_harness.dart). Need to verify the session JSONL actually records `model` on the assistant message record — `_foldAssistant` reads `message['model']` and byModel tests pass with model-a, so yes.

Wait — also check the mirror test (`SplitChannelCliIO`) — no changes needed there.

Now check: does the fa-tokens line in the existing CLI test 'a headless run leaves usage.json and one fa-tokens log line (AC1/AC7)' assert `usageTokensLogPattern.hasMatch(payload)` — with my change the payload will now contain `"model":"test-model"` — the UPDATED regex must match it. Yes, with the optional group.

